    "evidence_text": "Rapamycin passes the torch: a new generation of mTOR inhibitors\n\nGlutaminolysis activates Rag-mTORC1 signaling.\n\nAmino acids control cell growth via activation of the highly conserved kinase TORC1.\n\nGlutamine is a particularly important amino acid in cell growth control and metabolism.\n\nHowever, the role of glutamine in TORC1 activation remains poorly defined.\n\nThis may provide an explanation for glutamine addiction in cancer cells.\n\nHowever, therapeutic targeting of cancer metabolism has led to surprisingly few new drugs to date.\n\nThe Utilization of Extracellular Proteins as Nutrients Is Suppressed by mTORC1\n\nThese results may have important implications for the use of mTOR inhibitors as therapeutics.\n\nThe cystine-induced reduction in EPSCs was reversed by blocking either xc- or mGluR2/3.\n\nTOR Signaling in Growth and Metabolism\n\nmTORC2 Regulates Amino Acid Metabolism in Cancer by Phosphorylation of the Cystine-Glutamate Antiporter xCT.\n\nmTORC2 phosphorylates serine 26 at the cytosolic N terminus of xCT, inhibiting its activity.",
